{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789488211,"nonce":"8UxkJJYcFUnExpXOh89cRhcXGkc=","message":"Phase 1 landed in 81cd35d (amended: same tree).\n\nVerified: TestSimd byte-identical between stock hl 1.14 + simd.hdll and\nash interp / jit / hybrid / llvm-tier / AOT on the NUC, and between\ninterp / jit / hybrid / AOT on macOS arm64. cargo test simd_natives\nresolves all 133 primitives from the runtime with no library file.\n\nCodegen of the native bodies, read from the disassembly:\n\n- arm64 (NEON in the baseline): every lane primitive is a single vector\n  instruction between an unaligned load and store (fadd.4s, fmla.4s,\n  mul.4s, cmgt.16b, fcvtzs.4s, addv/smaxv for integer reductions).\n  IEEE min/max is ten NEON ops (fcmge/fcmgt/fcmeq + bif/bit); a single\n  fmin.4s would need llvm.minimum, i.e. f32::minimum, which is not on\n  stable. Float reductions are the pinned sequential fold (fadd x3).\n- x86-64: the crate targets baseline x86-64, so the bodies are compiled\n  twice and each export jumps through a per-primitive slot resolved on\n  first call (mov rax,[ENTRY]; jmp rax). Baseline: SSE2 (addps, pmuludq\n  dance for i32 mul, punpck/pmullw for i8 mul, fmaf calls per lane for\n  fma). AVX2+FMA (the NUC): vaddps, vfmadd213ps, vpmulld, vpminub +\n  vphminposuw, 256-bit vpmullw for i8 mul. Two stay scalar on x86:\n  f32x4_to_i32x4 (fptosi.sat lowers to cvttss2si + fixups per lane) and\n  the min/max *reductions* (pinned order). Neither is on a hot path in\n  the fixture; a vector formulation of the saturating convert is a\n  later item if it shows up in a benchmark.\n\nWhat phase 2 inherits: the LLVM JIT stamps host cpu+features on every\nfunction and Cranelift infers the host ISA (cranelift_native), so vector\nIR emitted for the intercepted calls will use AVX2/FMA on the NUC and\nNEON on arm64 without further plumbing; AOT native builds use the host\nCPU, cross builds are generic. The tiers today emit an ordinary call to\nhlp_\u003cname\u003e for every simd native; the IR to inspect for folding is\nphase 2's.\n\nHaxe compiler note pinned in the fixture: genhl pools float constants by\nvalue, so -0.0 and 0.0 literals in one module share a constant. Signed\nzero test lanes are written as bits.","files":null}]}